  "answer": "In humans, sex determination occurs through the combination of sex chromosomes. Each human cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes, with 22 pairs being autosomes (same in males and females) and the 23rd pair being allosomes or sex chromosomes. Females have two X chromosomes (XX), while males have one X and one Y chromosome (XY). During reproduction, the mother's gametes (ova) always carry an X chromosome, while the father's gametes (sperm) can carry either an X or a Y chromosome. If the sperm with an X chromosome fertilizes the ovum, the resulting zygote will have XX chromosomes and develop into a female. If the sperm with a Y chromosome fertilizes the ovum, the zygote will have XY chromosomes and develop into a male.",
